ce.  The Ordinal Placement for each judge was based on Total Points awarded by that judge to the skaters.  The points were based on 60% for Compulsory Figures and 40% for Free Skating.  Final placement was determined by a Majority Placement rule.  Thus, if a skater was ranked first by a majority of the judges, that skater was placed first overall, and the process was repeated for each place.  If no absolute majority for a place existed, the tiebreakers were, in order: 1) Total Ordinals, 2) Total Points, 3) Compulsory Figure Points."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003151"} "Kurt Dannenberg"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5001888"} "Eduard Engelmann"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003940"} "Paul Baudouin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/84794"} "Herbert Clarke"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/87331"} "Sakari Ilmanen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #6"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002822"} "Joel Liberman"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #7"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/98625"} "Vojtěch Veselý"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The men’s singles in 1928 was a repetition of the Grafström-Böckl duel from " [:a {:href "/results/12598"} "Chamonix"] ". " [:a {:href "/athletes/84539"} "Willy Böckl"] " had been the dominant skater in international Championships since 1924. He was crowned as both World and European Champion in 1925, 1926, and 1927. Three weeks before the Olympics, he won the European Championship in Opava, Czechoslovakia. However, " [:a {:href "/athletes/86175"} "Gillis Grafström"] " attended none of those Championships, as his work as an architect in Berlin prevented him from taking part."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In spite of a troublesome knee injury, Grafström was in the lead after the compulsory programme, and three days later he was also judged as the best free skater. He took his third Olympic figure skating individual gold, a feat which only " [:a {:href "/athletes/81280"} "Sonia Henie"] " has been able to repeat. Beaten by a scant margin, Böckl withdrew from skating after winning the World Championship later in the season, finishing his skating career with a total of 10 gold medals from World or European Championships, but he had to be content with two Olympic silver medals."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medalist, Belgian " [:a {:href "/athletes/98559"} "Robert Van Zeebroeck"] ", was an unmerited skater on the international scene. As a 16-year-old youngster, he was 7th in both the European and World Championships 1926. He impressed the St. Moritz audience with excellent leaps and spins, and two days later he competed in " [:a {:href "/results/12702"} "pair skating"] " with " [:a {:href "/athletes/82923"} "Josy Van Lerberghe"] ", placing 6th in the event. This would be his last championship appearance."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Placed fourth was another 18-year old youngster, Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/97368"} "Karl Schäfer"] ", who had finished in second place at the Europeans in Opava. The versatile young Viennese competed in " [:a {:href "/results/4877"} "swimming"] " at the Summer Games in Amsterdam later in the same year, until 2024 the only Olympian combining those quite different sports. He was at the beginning of an extraordinary figure skating career."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Majority Placements"] [:th {} "Total Ordinals"] [:th {} "Total Points"] [:th {} "Compulsory Figures"] [:th {} "Free Skating"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86175"} "Gillis Grafström"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "4×1+"] [:td {} "12.0"] [:td {} "2,698.25"] [:td {} "4×1+ (1)"] [:td {} "6×2+ (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "Points listed in 1928 Official Report for the total for all competitors are actually the points for the Compulsory Figures.", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "1"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/84539"} "Willy Böckl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href 